<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Delian League</h1><p class="st-meta">478 BCE – 404 BCE · capital Athens</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Athenian-led alliance formed against Persia in 478 BCE that turned into an Athenian empire funding the Parthenon and Athens' war with Sparta.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a>, the Ionian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> wanted to continue the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and resented the Spartan commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a>. In 478-477 BCE they swore an alliance with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> on the sacred island of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delos">Delos</a>. The Athenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristides">Aristides</a> assessed each member's contribution of ships or money, set at 460 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> a year.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Delegates first met on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delos">Delos</a>, where the treasury was kept, but <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> controlled the fleet and the treasurers. Over time most members paid tribute rather than providing ships, and revolts by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/naxos">Naxos</a> (c. 470 BCE), <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thasos">Thasos</a> (465 BCE) and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/samos">Samos</a> (440 BCE) were crushed. The treasury moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 454 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> used its funds for the buildings of the Acropolis. Athens imposed its coinage, courts and settlers on allies.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon's</a> victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-eurymedon">Eurymedon</a> (c. 466 BCE) secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. Rivalry with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, in which many allies revolted after the Sicilian disaster of 413 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>' surrender in 404 BCE dissolved the league.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/delian-league.jpg" alt="Delian League" width="320" height="256"><figcaption>Image: Map_Peloponnesian_War_431_BC-fr.svg: Marsyas derivative work: Aeonx (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Peloponnesian_War_431_BC-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>478 BCE – 404 BCE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Athens</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aristides, Cimon, Pericles</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>, <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Athenian Empire, Athenian League</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-441&amp;focus=delian-league" aria-label="Delian League on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5271 812 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Delian League lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5591 1068L5738 1122M5591 1068L5782 1103M5591 1068L5768 1117M5591 1068L5585 1076M5591 1068L5586 1053M5591 1068L5592 1055M5591 1068L5609 1067M5591 1068L5574 993M5591 1068L5574 996M5591 1068L5610 971M5591 1068L5605 978M5591 1068L5709 970M5591 1068L5685 990M5591 1068L5682 1083M5591 1068L5682 1070M5591 1068L5682 1079M5591 1068L5676 1064M5591 1068L5668 1061M5591 1068L5664 1046M5591 1068L5666 1056M5591 1068L5658 1055M5591 1068L56